# briizOS.Local Firestore Schema Map (`src/lib/firestore.js`)

This table reflects what `briizOS.Local` writes to Firestore today (primarily via `src/lib/firestore.js`).

| Firestore path | Fields written | Nomenclature notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `users/{uid}/panels/{noteId}` | `type` (`note`), `title`, `content`, `customColor` (`{h,s,l}`), `color`, `x`, `y`, `width`, `w`, `height`, `h`, `active`, `isDocked`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` | Dual size keys (`width` + `w`, `height` + `h`). Timestamp style is Firestore `serverTimestamp()`. |
| `users/{uid}/tasks/{taskId}` | `type`, `name`, `title`, `completed`, `status`, `priority`, `dueDate`, `parentId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `automated`, `ignored`, `taskLog[]`, `actions[]`, `source`, `automation` | Uses both `name` and `title`. In Local, `jobPanel` task docs can also act as message-first remote jobs, with visible logs and nested automation state on the same record. |
| `users/{uid}/events/{eventId}` | `type` (`event`), `name`, `title`, `description`, `scheduledDate`, `time`, `endTime`, `startMinute`, `durationMinutes`, `color`, `completed`, `allDay`, `projectId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` | Mixed time/date representation (`time` strings + minute integers). |
| `users/{uid}/organizer/{id}` (project flow) | `type` (`project`), `name`, `description`, `domain`, `status`, `completed`, `deadline`, `importance`, `size`, `relevance`, `tags[]`, `tasks[]`, `milestones[]`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` | Project writes use numeric `Date.now()` timestamps, not Firestore timestamp sentinels. |
| `users/{uid}/organizer/{id}` (organizer-item flow) | `type`, `name`, `body`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `scheduledDate`, `start`, `end`, `due`, `duration`, `completedAt`, `status`, `priority`, `tags[]`, `source`, `dateMode`, `dateValue`, `recurrence`, `owner`, `visibility`, `syncSource`, `lastModified`, `history[]` | Also numeric timestamps (`Date.now()`). Shares same collection as projects, so schema is polymorphic/disjoint. |
| `users/{uid}/desktopChatMessages/{msgId}` | `role`, `content`, `isError`, `timestamp` | Desktop-only chat log; `timestamp` is `serverTimestamp()`. |
| `users/{uid}/desktopSessions/{sessionId}` | `name`, `startTime`, `endTime`, `data` (notably `data.events[]`) | `data.events[]` entries use ISO string `timestamp` and arbitrary event payload in `data`. |
| `users/{uid}/desktopSelectedFiles/{fileId}` | `filePath`, `fileName`, `addedAt` | `fileId` is derived from base64(filePath) and sanitized. |
| `users/{uid}/desktopSettings/config` | Dynamic key/value settings via `{ [key]: value }` | Common keys from UI: `chatConfig`, `github_token`, `openai_api_key`, `gemini_api_key`, `claude_api_key`, `studio_video_url`, `studio_website_url`, `studio_replicate_url`, `studio_suno_url`, `studio_dalle_url`, `studio_ideogram_url`, `studio_myaudio_url`, `studio_briizclerk_url`. |
| `users/{uid}/desktopSettings/layout` | `config`, `updatedAt` | `config` typically contains `{ panels: [...], version }`. |
| `users/{uid}/desktopLogs/{logId}` | `action`, `message`, `timestamp` | Desktop-only operational logs. |
| `users/{uid}/desktopRemoteChat/{msgId}` | `role`, `content`, `timestamp`, `read` | Remote chat bridge collection; `read` toggled with merge writes. |
| `users/{uid}/desktopAgentJobs/{jobId}` | `id`, `type` (`tool`), `tool`, `params`, `opId`, `status`, `workerId`, `leaseUntil`, `retryCount`, `maxRetries`, `nextRunAt`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `finishedAt`, `result`, `lastError`, `source`, `createdAtServer`, `updatedAtServer` | Queue consumed by autonomous workers. Remote/web can enqueue by writing one doc. |
| `users/{uid}/desktopAgentWorkers/{workerId}` | `workerId`, `state`, `currentJobId`, `lastSeenAt`, `lastSeenAtServer` | Worker heartbeat/lease visibility. |
| `users/{uid}/desktopAgentToolOps/{opId}` | `opId`, `jobId`, `tool`, `status`, `startedAt`, `completedAt`, `result`, `error`, `startedAtServer`, `completedAtServer` | Idempotency ledger keyed by `opId`; reused result on duplicate ops. |
| `users/{uid}/desktopAgentEvents/{eventId}` | `type`, `payload`, `createdAt`, `createdAtServer` | Append-only execution/event log for tracing and UI timelines. |

Deletion behavior:
- `deleteDoc` is used for note/task/event/project/organizer/session/selected-file/remote-chat item deletion paths.
- Some clears use `writeBatch` deletes (`tasks` subtasks + parent, chat history clear, selected files clear).

## Verified Non-Firestore Persistence (Local App)

| Concern | Storage location | Key/schema |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Chat settings local cache | `localStorage` | `briizos2.chatSettings.v1` |
| Firestore SDK cache | IndexedDB (Firebase-managed) | Enabled via `initializeFirestore(...persistentLocalCache(...))` in `src/lib/firebase.js` |

Notes:
- Direct Firestore SDK calls are centralized in `src/lib/firestore.js`; other app code calls this layer.
- Timestamp conventions are mixed across flows (`serverTimestamp()` vs numeric `Date.now()`), including within the same top-level user namespace.

## Remote enqueue helper (web/online)

`src/lib/firestore.js` now exposes:
- `enqueueRemoteAgentToolJob(tool, params, options)` -> `{ jobId, opId }`
- `subscribeAgentJob(jobId, callback)` -> live status/result updates
- `subscribeAgentEvents(callback, maxItems)` -> live execution log

Minimal enqueue payload equivalent:
- Path: `users/{uid}/desktopAgentJobs/{jobId}`
- Required fields: `type: "tool"`, `tool`, `params`, `status: "pending"`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`
- Recommended fields: `opId`, `nextRunAt`, `retryCount`, `maxRetries`, `source`

## Message-first remote jobs

Local now also exposes a task-bucket path for remote jobs that should stay represented as one human request even when tools are used during execution:

- `createAutomatedJobTask(title, options)` -> creates a `users/{uid}/tasks/{taskId}` doc with `type: "jobPanel"`, `automated: true`, top-level task status, visible `taskLog[]`, `source`, and nested `automation` state.
- `claimNextAutomatedTask(options)` -> scans automated task docs and transactionally claims the next eligible job by setting `automation.runnerId`, `automation.leaseUntil`, and `automation.state`.
- `heartbeatAutomatedTask(taskId, options)` -> refreshes the lease on a claimed task.
- `updateAutomatedJobTask(taskId, updates)` -> merges task-level status fields and nested automation state without changing the job's canonical location.

This path is the intended model for "do this for me" requests. Tool use becomes an implementation detail recorded in the task log, not the primary queue record.
</file>

<file path="docs/persistence-analysis-web.md">
# briizos-dev (Web) — Persistence Analysis

## Architecture: Firestore-First, Single Page App

The web version is a vanilla JS app served via Firebase Hosting. No build system or npm dependencies for the core app — Firebase SDK is loaded via CDN (v10.12.4). All persistent data flows through Firestore.

---

## Persistence Mechanisms

### 1. Firestore (Primary — Cloud)

**Firebase Config:** Inline in `/public/index.html`
- Project: `briizos`
- Auth: email/password via Firebase Authentication
- Security Rules: **Development mode** (`allow read, write: if true;`)

**Firestore Structure:**

```
users/{uid}/
  ├── panels/{panelId}
  │   ├── id, type, title, content, tags
  │   ├── x, y (position), w, h (dimensions)
  │   ├── active, customColor {h, s, l}
  │   ├── externalUrl, externalData
  │   ├── chat {provider, model}
  │   ├── createdAt, updatedAt (serverTimestamp)
  │
  ├── tasks/{taskId}
  │   ├── id, name, completed, due, tag
  │   ├── createdAt, updatedAt
  │
  ├── events/{eventId}
  │   ├── id, name, scheduledDate, dateValue, recurrence
  │   ├── createdAt, updatedAt
  │
  ├── organizer/{itemId}
  │   ├── id, type, name, completed, tag
  │   ├── createdAt, updatedAt
  │
  └── app/
      └── minStack
          ├── value (array of minimized panel IDs)
          └── updatedAt (serverTimestamp)
```

**Migration:** V1 (`users/{uid}/snapshots/app` monolithic doc) → V2 (subcollections). Auto-migrates on first load.

### 2. localStorage (Secondary — Client)

| Key | Purpose | Active? |
|-----|---------|---------|
| `glOS.state.v1` | Full app state backup | **Disabled** (`USE_LOCAL_CACHE = false`) |
| `glOS.pending.v1` | Pending offline changes | Not active |
| `briizos.chatSettings.v1` | AI provider/model settings | Yes |
| `briizos.chatHistory.{panelId}` | Chat messages per panel (7-day auto-cleanup) | Yes |
| `gcal_user` | Google Calendar auth info | Yes |
| `gcal_auth_time` | Google Calendar auth timestamp | Yes |
| `GEMINI_API_KEY` | Gemini API key | Yes |
| `OPENAI_API_KEY` | OpenAI API key | Yes |
| `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` | Claude API key | Yes |

### 3. sessionStorage (Ephemeral)

| Key | Purpose |
|-----|---------|
| `cw-collapsed-{panelId}` | Chat panel collapse state (per tab) |

---

## State Management

**Centralized in-memory state object** (`window.state` / `window.appState`):
- `state.panels[]` — All UI panels
- `state.tasks[]` — Tasks/to-dos
- `state.events[]` — Calendar events
- `state.organizer[]` — Organizer items
- `state.panelMinStack[]` — Minimized panel IDs

**Supporting stores:**
- `window.OrganizerStore` — Tasks, events, organizer CRUD
- `window.settingsManager` — AI provider settings (up to 50 snapshots)
- `window.ChatWidget` — Chat history per panel
- `window.googleCalendarSync` — Google Calendar OAuth session
- `window.panelManager` — Panel lifecycle management

---

## Data Flow

### Write Path (UI → Firestore)

1. User action in UI
2. `state.panels[]` / `state.tasks[]` etc. updated in memory
3. `persistence.savePanel(panel)` called
4. `queueFirebaseSave()` adds to debounce queue (1000ms)
5. `flushSaveQueue()` sanitizes data → `setDoc()` with merge to Firestore
6. `serverTimestamp()` applied to `updatedAt`

### Read Path (Firestore → UI)

1. `onAuthStateChanged()` fires after login
2. `setupRealtimeSync(uid)` attaches `onSnapshot()` listeners to all collections
3. Firestore pushes real-time updates
4. `mergeArrayByIdInPlace()` updates in-memory state preserving object identity
5. `notifyUIUpdate(name)` triggers component re-renders

### Pre-Auth Queuing

- Writes queued before auth completes
- Retry with backoff every 2 seconds until `uid` available
- `flushAll()` called on `beforeunload` to ensure saves

---

## What's Persistent vs. Ephemeral

| Data | Firestore | localStorage | Ephemeral |
|------|-----------|--------------|-----------|
| Panels (notes, apps, timers) | ✅ | — | — |
| Panel position/size/color | ✅ | — | — |
| Tasks | ✅ | — | — |
| Events | ✅ | — | — |
| Organizer items | ✅ | — | — |
| Minimized panel stack | ✅ | — | — |
| AI model/provider settings | — | ✅ | — |
| Chat history (7-day TTL) | — | ✅ | — |
| Google Calendar session | — | ✅ | — |
| API keys | — | ✅ | — |
| Chat collapse state | — | — | ✅ (sessionStorage) |
| Scroll positions | — | — | ✅ |
| Search results | — | — | ✅ |
| Undo/redo stacks | — | — | ✅ |

---

## Key Characteristics

- **Firestore is the authoritative source** for all core app data
- **No offline mode** — app requires Firestore connectivity for data
- **Debounced writes** (1000ms) minimize API calls
- **Real-time sync** via `onSnapshot()` listeners
- **No build step** — vanilla JS, Firebase via CDN
- **Security rules are wide open** (`allow read, write: if true;`) — development mode
</file>

<file path="docs/REMOTE_JOB_FIRESTORE_REQUIREMENTS.md">
# Remote Job Firestore Requirements

This document maps the Firestore requirements for the message-first remote job model.

## Goal

A remote request like "text Roy the file on my desktop" should live as one job record from start to finish.

Whether the local agent uses zero tools, one shell command, five tool calls, or gets blocked by permissions should not change where that job is represented.

## Core rule

The canonical record for a remote "do this for me" request is a task doc in:

`users/{uid}/tasks/{taskId}`

Specifically:

- `type: "jobPanel"`
- `automated: true`
- user request stored in `title`
- user-visible timeline stored in `taskLog[]`
- execution metadata stored in nested `automation`

Tool tickets are not the canonical representation for this workflow.

## Bucket requirements

### 1. Canonical job bucket

Path:

`users/{uid}/tasks/{taskId}`

Why:

- already visible in existing job/task UI
- already supports `taskLog[]`
- already supports subtasks/steps through `actions[]`
- already has `status`, `completed`, `ignored`, and timestamps

Required fields for message-first remote jobs:

- `id`
- `type: "jobPanel"`
- `title`
- `status`
- `automated: true`
- `ignored: false`
- `createdAt`
- `updatedAt`
- `taskLog[]`
- `automation`
- `source`

Recommended nested `automation` shape:

- `mode: "message-first"`
- `state: "queued" | "running" | "waiting_for_user" | "blocked" | "completed" | "failed" | "cancelled"`
- `runnerId`
- `workerId`
- `threadId`
- `leaseUntil`
- `claimedAt`
- `lastHeartbeatAt`
- `nextRunAt`
- `currentAction`
- `currentTool`
- `awaitingUser`
- `blockedReason`
- `lastError`
- `createdBy`

### 2. User-visible log

Path:

Embedded on the same task doc as `taskLog[]`

Why:

- keeps the human request and its execution history together
- matches the existing task/job card UI
- supports entries like `status`, `question`, `response`, `error`, `tool`

Example entries:

- "Job queued from web"
- "Claimed by local runner"
- "Checking Desktop for task.txt"
- "Contacts permission denied"
- "Waiting for user response"

### 3. Step breakdown

Path:

Embedded on the same task doc as `actions[]`

Why:

- existing task system already knows how to display and persist step lists
- lets the agent break the request into visible subtasks without creating a second queue record

### 4. Worker heartbeat bucket

Path:

`users/{uid}/desktopAgentWorkers/{workerId}`

Why:

- operational visibility is still useful
- worker liveness should remain separate from the human job record

This bucket is optional for UX, but useful for debugging and claims.

### 5. Optional low-level event bucket

Path:

`users/{uid}/desktopAgentEvents/{eventId}`

Why:

- good for ops/debugging
- not required for the user-facing job lifecycle

This should remain secondary. The primary user story should still be understandable from the task doc plus `taskLog[]`.

## Non-requirements

These are not required for the canonical remote-job model:

- a separate tool-ticket queue per user request
- a separate "job response" bucket if replies can be represented as task log entries plus status changes
- a requirement that every job use tools

## Status model

Top-level task status should remain human-readable and UI-friendly.

Suggested top-level values:

- `queued`
- `active`
- `waiting`
- `completed`
- `failed`
- `cancelled`

Suggested nested `automation.state` values:

- `queued`
- `running`
- `waiting_for_user`
- `blocked`
- `completed`
- `failed`
- `cancelled`
- `needs_retry`

Rule of thumb:

- top-level `status` is for the human-facing job card
- nested `automation.state` is for runner/engine control

## Queue semantics

The queue is not a tool list.

The queue is a list of human requests represented as automated `jobPanel` task docs.

The local runner should:

1. claim one task doc
2. mark it active/running
3. run the main agent against that request
4. append logs and step updates back onto that same task doc
5. set waiting/completed/failed on that same task doc

## Current implementation direction

The Local Firestore layer now has foundational helpers for this model:

- `createAutomatedJobTask(...)`
- `appendTaskLogEntry(...)`
- `updateAutomatedJobTask(...)`
- `claimNextAutomatedTask(...)`
- `heartbeatAutomatedTask(...)`

These helpers intentionally keep the canonical job in `tasks`, even if tools are used during execution.
</file>

<file path="electron/agent-config.js">
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const { app } = require('electron');

const APP_SUPPORT = path.join(app.getPath('appData'), 'briizOS');

const DIRS = {
  root: APP_SUPPORT,
  canvas: path.join(APP_SUPPORT, 'canvas'),
  memory: path.join(APP_SUPPORT, 'memory'),
  chatLogs: path.join(APP_SUPPORT, 'memory', 'chat-logs'),
};

const CONFIG_PATH = path.join(APP_SUPPORT, 'config.json');
const AGENT_CONTEXT_PATH = path.join(APP_SUPPORT, 'memory', 'agent-context.md');

function ensureDirectories() {
  for (const dir of Object.values(DIRS)) {
    fs.mkdirSync(dir, { recursive: true });
  }
  if (!fs.existsSync(CONFIG_PATH)) {
    fs.writeFileSync(CONFIG_PATH, JSON.stringify({
      filesystem: { blacklist: { paths: [], filetypes: [] } },
      terminal: { mode: 'ask-always' },
      memory: { chunksToInclude: 1 },
    }, null, 2));
  }
  if (!fs.existsSync(AGENT_CONTEXT_PATH)) {
    fs.writeFileSync(AGENT_CONTEXT_PATH, '# Agent Memory\n\nNo memories stored yet.\n');
  }
}

function readConfig() {
  try {
    return JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(CONFIG_PATH, 'utf8'));
  } catch {
    return {
      filesystem: { blacklist: { paths: [], filetypes: [] } },
      terminal: { mode: 'ask-always' },
      memory: { chunksToInclude: 1 },
    };
  }
}

function writeConfig(config) {
  fs.writeFileSync(CONFIG_PATH, JSON.stringify(config, null, 2));
}

function updateConfig(partial) {
  const config = readConfig();
  // Deep merge for nested objects
  for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(partial)) {
    if (typeof value === 'object' && value !== null && !Array.isArray(value) && typeof config[key] === 'object') {
      config[key] = { ...config[key], ...value };
    } else {
      config[key] = value;
    }
  }
  writeConfig(config);
  return config;
}

module.exports = {
  APP_SUPPORT, DIRS, CONFIG_PATH, AGENT_CONTEXT_PATH,
  ensureDirectories, readConfig, writeConfig, updateConfig,
};
